#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101596} :activity/id #uuid "d9f6c59e-6046-554a-a0f2-8e258f0aa5ae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048666 :topic/id #uuid "b35faa98-a789-50da-a860-eef410538855" :topic/math-academy-id 1258} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101940 :tutorial/content "How can we find *all* the solutions to the trigonometric equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}?\n$$\n\nNotice that a domain has not been specified. This means that we should assume the domain $-\\infty < x < \\infty.$ In such cases, there are usually infinitely many solutions. The expression that generates all possible solutions to a trigonometric equation like this is called the **general solution.** \n\nTo find the general solution, we perform the following two steps:\n\n- Find two different solutions $\\color{red} x_1$ and $\\color{blue} x_2$ in the domain $[0, 2\\pi).$\n\n- Write down the general solution as\n\n$$\nx = {\\color{red} x_1}+2n\\pi, \\quad x={\\color{blue} x_2}+2n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere the number $n$ is assumed to be any integer. This just means that the general solution consists of all angles that are coterminal to $\\color{red}x_1$ or ${\\color{blue}x_2}.$\n\nIn our example, we find the principal value as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis first solution lies in the first quadrant and has reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin x >0,$ a second solution must lie in the second quadrant and also have reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](images/00/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution $\\color{blue}x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x \\in [0 , 2\\pi)$ are ${\\color{red}x_1}={\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}},$ ${\\color{blue}x_2}={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}}.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is \n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}}+2n\\pi, \\quad x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}}+2n\\pi .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e808624a-a333-59f7-9f93-9d7d7a9b2b1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2577 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33b2e98c-3ad1-5336-88e5-5954345d4de1" :step/math-academy-id 11966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100653 :question/id #uuid "b52808fa-1f08-53ac-a5e0-97dd05b1e57b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3552"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051050}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302107}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "e4b8c147-a30c-526c-9fac-b27523d8000a" :step/math-academy-id 11967 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101941 :tutorial/content "The function $\\tan x$ has a period that is equal to $180^\\circ.$ This fact makes it very easy to construct the general solution to a trigonometric equation containing tangent. \n\nTo find the general solution of an equation like $\\tan x = \\alpha,$ we perform the following steps:\n\n- Find the principal value ${\\color{blue}x_1} = \\arctan \\alpha.$\n\n- Write down the general solution as\n\n$$\nx= {\\color{blue}x_1} + n\\cdot 180^\\circ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nAnd that's all there is to it!\n\nFor instance, let's find the general solution of the equation\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x_1} = \\arctan\\left({\\sqrt{3}}\\right)= {\\color{blue}60^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $180^\\circ.$  Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = {\\color{blue}60^\\circ} +n\\cdot 180^\\circ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "00e8b172-c0c1-5f58-94af-86c000797572" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2578 :tutorial/title "Finding General Solutions of Elementary Trigonometric Equations Containing Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "8ba91d5b-5336-5f19-8cbe-1eac6932e986" :step/math-academy-id 11968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100654 :question/id #uuid "6831fce9-9c10-5c75-9431-529b852c9bed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050313}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345249}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"} :step/id #uuid "d7b30917-721e-550b-948e-76a816f15b74" :step/math-academy-id 11969 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592